ld/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-22a7b6b1-52cf-5ddb-acbb-0f5547ee59d4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9fd12201-5c70-544a-840c-8deb4a3ddc39" "answer-ad51386c-e6a1-55f0-b241-01c1795a3521" "answer-22a7b6b1-52cf-5ddb-acbb-0f5547ee59d4" "answer-41fda493-83ab-5115-880f-cdc2ef314413" "answer-6ba0118d-ddbf-5d11-ab83-471b45b97b1e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fd12201-5c70-544a-840c-8deb4a3ddc39" :answer/id #uuid "9fd12201-5c70-544a-840c-8deb4a3ddc39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{39}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad51386c-e6a1-55f0-b241-01c1795a3521" :answer/id #uuid "ad51386c-e6a1-55f0-b241-01c1795a3521" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{41}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22a7b6b1-52cf-5ddb-acbb-0f5547ee59d4" :answer/id #uuid "22a7b6b1-52cf-5ddb-acbb-0f5547ee59d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{44}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41fda493-83ab-5115-880f-cdc2ef314413" :answer/id #uuid "41fda493-83ab-5115-880f-cdc2ef314413" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{48}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ba0118d-ddbf-5d11-ab83-471b45b97b1e" :answer/id #uuid "6ba0118d-ddbf-5d11-ab83-471b45b97b1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e0ae28b7-07ff-5372-8c05-3a6fb2ad772e" :question/id #uuid "e0ae28b7-07ff-5372-8c05-3a6fb2ad772e" :question/math-academy-id "q-171825" :question/problem "![](images/89/89a4eb747c82276f349350f48ac2b711e1a308702eb1c717fbeacf79dbdad84f.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $m∠BAE={185}^{∘},$ $m∠BAD={120}^{∘},$ $m∠CAD={70}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$m∠CAE=$\n{{field-1}} ${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "The measure of $∠DAE$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(∠BAE)$ and the smaller known angle $(∠BAD).$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nm∠DAE={185}^{∘}-{120}^{∘}={65}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add this to our diagram:\n\n![](images/fb/fb782eecfe7280c5808c8f0a42dbe0daec4110d94aa373271a03311dd0a0ead0.png)\n\nWe can now find the measure of $∠CAE:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠CAE & =m∠CAD+m∠DAE \\\\  & ={70}^{∘}+{65}^{∘} \\\\  & ={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15bea92c-fa20-58d1-9617-d5a73063267b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15bea92c-fa20-58d1-9617-d5a73063267b" :answer-field/id #uuid "15bea92c-fa20-58d1-9617-d5a73063267b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3f249e0c-668b-5be1-b8ff-16f87f4d5391" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3f249e0c-668b-5be1-b8ff-16f87f4d5391"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f249e0c-668b-5be1-b8ff-16f87f4d5391" :answer/id #uuid "3f249e0c-668b-5be1-b8ff-16f87f4d5391" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "135" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111836 :tutorial/content "When two angles share a common side, they are called **adjacent** angles. When two angles are adjacent, we can add their measures to find the measure of the larger angle. \n\nFor example, consider the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f5/f562f3505de0dd2345b29e2baabaf51a7885cbccb5d95e6201b8bc9aec1b1986.png)\n\nThe angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent because they both have the side $\\overrightarrow{SR}$ in common.\n\nSince the angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent, we can find the measure of the combined angle $\\angle PST$ by adding the two angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠PST & ={{m∠PSR}_{⏟}}_{\\text{orange angle}}+{{m∠RST}_{⏟}}_{\\text{blue angle}} \\\\  & ={95}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={140}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111899 :tutorial/content "We can extend our rule relating sums of adjacent angles to more than two angles.\n\nFor example, let's consider the angles shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/32/3264d0ab1a40b02915a737dad57e9f26820193d9bdc6b42431ec1ab6508cc523.png)\n\nAccording to our rule, the measure of the reflex angle $\\angle QST$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QST & ={{m∠QSP}_{⏟}}_{\\text{green angle}}+{{m∠PSR}_{⏟}}_{\\text{orange angle}}+{{m∠RST}_{⏟}}_{\\text{blue angle}} \\\\  & ={100}^{∘}+{95}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use this rule to determine the size of a smaller angle when we know a larger one. Let's see an example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110861 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
